  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nIf you look out your window to the west and squint hard enough, \nyou may be able to see the broad cluster of storms ongoing early\nthis morning across the Dakotas. These storms are developing \nwithin an area of WAA along the eastern edge of the nocturnal \nLLJ. As the morning progresses, expect the storms to gradually \nmove east into parts of western MN, though they may be waning as\nthe LLJ weakens by mid morning. A couple thousand Joules of \nMLCAPE and effective wind shear of about 35 kts could support \nsome organization of storms, and result in isolated cases of \nsevere wind or hail, particularly across southwest MN.\n\nOverall heat impacts will be lower today with temperatures in \nthe 80s and dewpoints largely in the 60s. Southwest MN may still\nfeel muggy with 70 degree dewpoints lingering into today.\n\nThis pattern remains the same for the rest of the holiday\nweekend, with the ridge aloft only just starting to break down \nby Monday. Our best chance for more widespread precip will be \ntied to the shortwave that moves through Monday into Tuesday, \nthough northern Minnesota remains favored for the best forcing \nand greatest rainfall potential. The SPC has a marginal risk for\nsevere weather over western MN for the next three days while \nthis hot and stormy pattern holds. \n\nThe rest of the week will feature increased NW/W flow, ushering \nin a more seasonable airmass for early September.",
